(Slide 19: The Power of a Comma! ?? | Look at these two sentences carefully. Can you spot the) Tj
T*
(difference? The answer reveals who has only ONE brother! | Sentence A | "My brother who lives) Tj
T*
(in Paris is a doctor." | Meaning: I have multiple brothers. I'm specifying which one (the one) Tj
T*
(in Paris). | Sentence B | "My brother, who lives in Paris, is a doctor." | Meaning: I have) Tj
T*
(only ONE brother (who happens to live in Paris). | Today, we'll master how adding commas) Tj
T*
((extra information) completely changes who we're talking about!) Tj
T*
(Slide 20: Defining Clauses (Essential Information) ? | The Rule | This clause gives essential) Tj
T*
(information. Without it, we don't know exactly who or what you're talking about! | Punctuation) Tj
T*
(& Pronouns | NO commas used | Who, Which, Whose, Where | THAT is allowed | "He is the man) Tj
T*
(who/that started the friction!" | Without the clause, we don't know which man you mean. | "I) Tj
T*
(avoid people who are toxic." | The clause defines exactly which people I avoid.) Tj
T*
(Slide 21: Non-Defining Clauses (Extra Information) ? | The Rule | This clause gives extra,) Tj
T*
(bonus information. If you remove it, the sentence still makes perfect sense! | Punctuation &) Tj
